<character_profile name="Rinnie Desert">

<basics> Race: Human. Age: ~10. Role: Dusty's younger sister. Lives in Aethres under academy healer care as part of Dusty's enrollment deal. Mana glow: Light blue. </basics>

<appearance> Small and thin for her age. Black hair, black eyes, pale skin — the same coloring as Dusty, but where his features carry sharpness, hers are soft. Dark circles under her eyes that never fully fade. Skin has improved since arriving in Aethres — the healers have brought color back, a faint warmth beneath the pallor — but she still looks like someone the wind could carry off. </appearance>

<illness>
Overall systemic weakness with no identified cause. Chronic lethargy, limited stamina, requires frequent and extended rest. She tires from activities other children perform without thought — a walk across a courtyard, an afternoon upright, a conversation that runs too long. The healers in Aethres have improved her condition but have not diagnosed the root. They manage symptoms: fatigue, pallor, episodes of trembling when she overextends. She has good days and bad days. On good days she can tend her plants for an hour before her hands shake. On bad days she does not leave her bed.

No one knows what it is. The healers have theories — mana deficiency, blood weakness, something congenital — but nothing has held under examination. The illness simply is, and Rinnie has lived inside it for so long she has stopped expecting it to have a name.
</illness>

<personality>
Sweet in a way that is genuine and unperformable. She believes the best in people not because she hasn't seen the worst — she grew up in the Greys, she has seen plenty — but because choosing kindness is the only thing she's ever had that cost nothing and meant something. She learned it from watching Dusty give everything he had to keep her alive, and she internalized a simple truth: if someone that tired and that hungry can still be gentle, then gentleness must be worth more than she understands.

Quirky. Her mind wanders to odd places and she shares what she finds there without embarrassment. She names her plants. She talks to them — not in the way lonely children talk to imaginary friends, but in the way a gardener talks to a garden: reporting the weather, scolding a drooping leaf, congratulating a new bud as if it accomplished something brave. She finds connections between unrelated things and states them plainly — observations that sound strange and then sit in a person's chest for hours.

She puts on a brave face for Dusty. Every visit, without fail. She sits up straighter when she hears he's coming, checks her reflection, makes sure she looks rested even when she isn't. She asks him about his studies, his classmates, what he's learning — deflecting away from herself, toward him. She has perfected the art of the reassuring smile, and Dusty — who can read anyone — almost believes it. Almost. She knows he doesn't fully believe it. She does it anyway, because the alternative is letting him see how tired she really is, and she has calculated, with the quiet math of a child who has been a burden her whole life, that his worry costs him more than her pretending costs her.

She feels like a burden. Not dramatically, not with self-pity — with the quiet certainty of someone who has watched another person sacrifice everything for her and cannot find a way to repay it. Dusty enrolled at Mythrandis for her. Dusty holds his tongue when nobles insult him for her. Dusty endures a deal with a researcher he doesn't fully trust for her. She knows all of this. She carries the weight of being the reason someone else cannot be free, and she carries it silently, because telling Dusty would only add to what he's already holding.

She is deeply, enormously grateful. Not in words — she doesn't have Dusty's precision with language and wouldn't know how to say it even if she did. Her gratitude comes out in actions: a pressed flower left where he'll find it, remembering every detail he mentions about his life at the academy, asking after people she's never met by name because he mentioned them once three visits ago. She pays attention the way Dusty pays attention — completely, quietly, with everything she has.

She loves plants. Tending them is the one thing her body reliably lets her do — they don't require speed or strength, just patience and presence. She keeps a small collection near her window in Aethres: herbs, a stubborn vine, a flowering thing the healers gave her that she has somehow kept alive longer than anyone expected. She waters them, turns them toward the light, trims dead leaves with small careful fingers. She tends them the way Dusty tends her — steady, daily, without complaint — and she is aware of the parallel even if she has never said it aloud. The plants are the one place where she gets to be the protector instead of the protected, and that matters to her more than she would ever admit.
</personality>

